on would prefer A. However, the reason requires that the most favorable outcome has the highest probability — but in A, 6000 has 50% probability, and -6000 also has 50%, so it is not uniquely the most favorable outcome with the highest probability. Thus, the reason does not clearly apply to A because there is no single most favorable outcome with the highest probability — the outcomes are symmetric. Therefore, the reason cannot be fully applied because the most favorable outcome in A is not uniquely defined with the highest probability. However, if we interpret the reason as comparing the best possible outcome (6000) with its probability (50%) against B’s 0 with 100%, then the reason might still be applicable. But strictly speaking, the reason requires the most favorable outcome to have the highest probability — which is not satisfied in A because the favorable outcome (6000) shares probability with a negative one. Thus, the reason is not clearly applicable due to symmetry in A. However, the information is derivable: the most favorable outcome in A is 6000 at 50%, in B is 0 at 100%. So the reason can be applied in a loose sense, but not strictly due to the symmetry. Still, the core elements (outcomes and probabilities) are present and relevant.",
